* The authors' DESeq blocks are on a different count scale than the supplied normalized matrix
  (median baseMean ratio baseline_blood 0.97, dentate_gyrus 1.72, final_blood 0.75). A constant per-tissue scale does not
  change log2FC, but it changes the Poisson/NB information: at the supplied scale the NB2 dispersion is larger and the
  Wald statistics much smaller. Their smallest p-values are per tissue baseline_blood 1e-190, dentate_gyrus 5e-69, final_blood 4e-119 vs baseline_blood 1e-10, dentate_gyrus 5e-13, final_blood 1e-07 for the GLM
  (median GLM p among the 194 dentate-gyrus workbook hits: 0.00277,
  with 172/194 at raw p < 0.05 - i.e. the GLM
  sees the same signals but multiplicity correction removes them).
* We cannot reproduce the authors' per-gene "exclude outliers" step (which samples/counts were dropped is not in the
  workbook), so per-gene dispersion and hence p-values cannot be matched exactly.
* Implementation check: the vectorised NB2 GLM was validated against `statsmodels.NegativeBinomial` (NB2 ML) on
  113 dentate-gyrus genes - log2FC correlation 0.99999999,
  median |dlog2FC| = 3.9e-08, p-value Spearman 0.9989
  (`results/statsmodels_validation_subset.csv`).

* Literal bookkeeping: one final-blood gene has `log2FoldChange = +Inf` in the workbook (zero counts in every control sample); `Inf > 0.5` satisfies the stated rule, so it is counted (final blood 15 rather than 14). Excluding it does not change any ranking.

