| raw p<0.05 & lfc>0.5 & baseMean>10 | 125 | 288 | 77 | 43 | 139 | 9 |


The dentate gyrus is the maximum in **every** variant for the answer source **and** for the GLM cross-check -
literal rule ({'baseline_blood': 88, 'dentate_gyrus': 194, 'final_blood': 15} vs {'baseline_blood': 0, 'dentate_gyrus': 2, 'final_blood': 0}), abs(lfc) > 0.5 with and without the baseMean floor, the stricter
log2(1.5) fold-change cut, unadjusted p < 0.05 ({'baseline_blood': 125, 'dentate_gyrus': 288, 'final_blood': 77} workbook / {'baseline_blood': 43, 'dentate_gyrus': 139, 'final_blood': 9} GLM, which widens the gap),
and the variant that drops the expression floor entirely ({'baseline_blood': 206, 'dentate_gyrus': 227, 'final_blood': 19} workbook / {'baseline_blood': 2, 'dentate_gyrus': 65, 'final_blood': 0} GLM).
The answer therefore does not depend on how "significantly" or which side of the fold-change is read.

## The one conflicting reading, stated openly
The workbook also carries undocumented `DEG` flag columns ("Max" / "Median"). Counting their `U` calls for the
bad-responder comparisons gives baseline blood 203 >
dentate gyrus 183 > final blood 18 - the opposite ranking to the question.
Those flags do **not** apply `baseMean > 10`: decoding them shows |log2FC| > log2(1.5) ~ 0.585 with padj < 0.05 plus a
*max/median expression* filter (they are reproducible with `padj<0.05 & |lfc|>0.585 & max(tissue expression) > 10`,
not with baseMean), i.e. they answer a different question. Under every rule that actually applies the requested
`baseMean > 10` filter, the dentate gyrus leads (194 vs 88 literal; 288 vs 125 at raw p).

